# Datamodel Enforcement

For various reasons, it is useful to split code from data model. Both physicl and logical ones.

For example, it can be a different team working on it. Or it can be hallucination prevention. In case AI goes nuts and starts to simplify datamodel, which is effectively service-to-service contracts.

Policy should effectively help humans and AIs to work on code.

Example A:
Website is not allowed to modify it’s datamodel. It is set on the backend. Either OpenAPI or TS Datamodel is used to distribute it. Or maybe even GraphQL. Doesn’t matter.

However. AI might want to have additions.

So Production code expects only certain types to be defined within project:

${Something}Props – for React props.
TestType${Something} – Test types helpers.

Everything else being passed down or generated.

However we don't want to complicate development.

So AI is allowed to define temporary classes here and there. Maybe to later ask edits.

And it is allowed to define Z_TempClass_{String} or to be precise ${UglyPrefix}_{String} 

types that it might need to use for dev time.